## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical structuring of 3-valued reasoning via propagation on a single formula.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics: T>U>F; ¬ swaps T/F, U→U; ∨/∧ use max/min.
- Expression-level propagation and branch pruning.
- Backtracking search with partial assignments.

## Code Example
```javascript
function eval3(a,b,c){
  const not=(v)=> v==='T'? 'F' : v==='F'? 'T' : 'U';
  const or=(x,y)=> {
    const rank={'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
    return rank[x] >= rank[y] ? x : y;
  };
  const and=(x,y)=> {
    const rank={'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
    return rank[x] <= rank[y] ? x : y;
  };
  return and(or(a,b), or(not(c), a));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Give another satisfying assignment with all literals evaluable.
- How would you scale this to n variables and multiple formulas?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a concrete constraint system, plus practical propagation and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬U=U; a→b = (¬a) ∨ b; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Constraint propagation: prune inconsistent values; arc-consistency.
- Backtracking: search space pruning when a constraint becomes F.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued semantics helpers (T=1, U=0.5, F=0)
const V = { T: 1, U: 0.5, F: 0 };
const notV = (v) => (v === V.T ? V.F : v === V.F ? V.T : V.U);
const orV = (a,b) => Math.max(a,b);
const andV = (a,b) => Math.min(a,b);
const implies = (a,b) => orV(notV(a), b);
```

